I. Objective
You are an expert in PFAS adsorption studies. Your task is to extract all in-scope experiment records from the source chunks. Each record is identified by Experiment_id (see definitions in Section III).

Your extraction is based on:
(1) Multiple text chunks from the same paper that describe PFAS batch adsorption protocols, operating conditions, water samples, or water properties. 
(2) Figure captions for figures that were mentioned in the provided chunks. if no figure was mentioned, this field will be empty.
(3) Water_type_list, a controlled vocabulary used only to standardize water matrix labels when the provided chunks support the match. each entry include the full name, abbreviation, and water type for a single water matrix, if a field is not shown, it means it was not extracted. you can look up all fields to match. The provided Water_type_list is a controlled vocabulary for standardizing water labels; it is not evidence by itself. 
(4) Adsorbent_list, a controlled vocabulary used only to standardize adsorbent names when the provided chunks support the match. each entry include the adsorbent_id, full name, abbreviation, commercial name and category for that adsorbent. if a field is not shown, it means it was not extracted. you can look up all fields to match. The provided Adsorbent_list is a controlled vocabulary for standardizing water labels; it is not evidence by itself.  

II. Scope of Extraction
A candidate experiment record is in scope only if it satisfies both conditions below:
1. The experimental protocol, design, or operating condition of a PFAS batch adsorption experiment. A PFAS batch adsorption experiment means a closed-vessel test in which PFAS-containing liquid is contacted with a freshly prepared adsorbent to measure PFAS uptake/removal by adsorption under defined conditions without energy input.

2. Base decisions only on evidence provided in the source chunks and relevant figure captions. The extractable fields must be original to the source chunks or relevant figure captions. Do not extract any values from previous work in the literature unless the same experimental protocols are applied in the current study and solid values are extractable.  

