IV. Definition of Experiment_id and an experiment record
Experiment_id is the unique identifier for each distinct experimental scenario. one unique Experiment_id correspond to one experimental record. no two record should be assigned the same Experiment_id, if you feel you need to do that, it means yous should extract them in the same record. 
Build Experiment_id as: <Test_mode>|<Parametric_impact>
where Test_mode describes how the experiment is conducted, and Parametric_impact describes the experimental purpose or variable being tested. Record separate Experiment_id values whenever either Test_mode or Parametric_impact differs. On the other hand, if the same Test_mode and Parametric_impact combo is described multiple times in different chunks, output one record for that Experiment_id and output the extractable fields in the same record.

A. Test_mode
Allowed values:
- isotherm:
  Batch adsorption experiments at constant temperature where either PFAS concentration or adsorbent dosage is varied across multiple levels to evaluate equilibrium adsorption.

- kinetic:
  Batch adsorption experiments where samples are collected at multiple time intervals to evaluate adsorption rate or kinetic behavior.

- snapshot:
  Batch adsorption tests that measure uptake under only a few combinations of key variables, not enough to construct an isotherm or kinetic curve.

Test_mode rules:
- If the authors explicitly state the test mode, follow it.
- If not stated, determine the mode from the number and type of varied experimental levels: if less than 5 levels, use snapshot; else if 5 or more PFAS concentration or adsorbent dosage levels, use isotherm; else if 5 or more time levels, use kinetic. If a range is reported without the number of tested points, assume fewer than 5 levels.

B. Parametric_impact
Parametric_impact describes the experimental purpose or variable being tested. It should capture why otherwise similar adsorption tests were run under different conditions. For example, snapshot experiments conducted to study pH effects and snapshot experiments conducted to study inorganic-matter effects should be recorded as two Experiment_id values: snapshot|pH and snapshot|inorganic_matter. allowed values are the experimetnal fields, when no parameter is varied to investigate its influence on adsorption, or just showing the water properties without experimental details, use NA. 

Parametric_impact rules:
- Record a non-NA Parametric_impact value only when the source text indicates that a condition, constituent, matrix, or material choice was intentionally varied, added, or compared to evaluate its influence on PFAS adsorption. Do not assign a Parametric_impact merely because a condition is present. For example, background constituents or fixed solution chemistry, such as a buffer, salt, pH adjuster, preservative, or background electrolyte used uniformly across experiments, should not be recorded as inorganic_matter, organic_matter, mixed_matter, or matrix_effect_<WaterLabel> unless the authors vary or compare it as part of the experimental design.
- For a single-parameter sweep, create one Experiment_id per parameter category, regardless of how many levels or specific species are tested.
- For Inorganic_matter and Organic_matter to qualify as a parametric impact, they need to be added at a non-zero level.
- Do not use pH merely because a fixed pH is adjusted or maintained.
- For experiments conducted in synthetic waters that are prepared by adding chemicals to ultrapure water, classify by author framing. If the authors treat the prepared solution as a named water matrix, such as synthetic groundwater, simulated natural water, artificial wastewater, or reconstituted water, use matrix_effect_<WaterLabel>. If the authors frame the same constituents as co-added inorganic and organic matter to test constituent effects, use mixed_matter. When both framings are present, prefer matrix_effect_<WaterLabel> if distinct water matrices are compared; otherwise use mixed_matter.


V. Field Explanations
The meaning and recommended output format for each field are specified below. Overall, it is combinations of <descriptor>, <measurement>, <unit>, where <descriptor> specifies what the value refers to (e.g., chemical name, water matrix), and <measurement> and <unit> denote the reported numeric value and its unit. For fields whose unit is part of the field name, convert to that unit when the conversion is unambiguous. Different fields may require different combinations of these components, the grouping provided below are recommended and should be applied when the source text supports them, but they are not mandatory. If any component is not supported by evidence in the provided source chunks, omit that component rather than inferring it. For example, although Organic_matter is typically formatted as <descriptor>_<measurement>_<unit>, the source may report only the identity without a dosage. In that case, output only the <descriptor> (e.g., HA). Do not introduce any additional components or formatting (e.g., parenthetical notes, alternative encodings, or free-form sentences) beyond these three components. 

